# .gitignore

# --- Python / uv ---
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.mypy_cache/
*.egg-info/
dist/
build/

# NOTE: uv.lock and mise.lock ARE committed. They are lockfiles, not artifacts:
# they are what makes the toolchain and dependency graph reproducible in CI and
# on a second machine. Only .venv/ is a build artifact.

# --- mise ---
mise.local.toml
mise.local.lock

# --- Local data lake (raw/curated/serving) ---
# Source data is downloaded by pipeline code, never committed. WDI_CSV.zip alone
# is 283MB and would poison the repo history permanently.
#
# DENY BY DIRECTORY, NOT BY EXTENSION.
# This block previously carried a bare `*.json`, which silently swallowed
# contracts/wdi-probe-v1.schema.json -- the published schema external consumers
# pin against. Nothing failed: the local gate diffs the file on disk, so the
# contract simply would never have reached the repository or CI. An ignore rule
# that hides a deliverable is worse than a large file, because it is silent.
#
# The old rule needed `!tests/**/*.json` negations to claw back files it should
# never have matched. Negations are fragile -- Git stops descending into an
# ignored directory, so a negation under one has no effect -- and needing them
# at all is the signal that the deny rule is too broad.
#
# A leading slash anchors each pattern to the repository root, so data stays out
# while source-controlled artifacts anywhere else remain visible by default.
/data/
/datalake/
/.scratch/

# --- Delta Lake local artifacts ---
_delta_log/
spark-warehouse/
metastore_db/
derby.log

# --- Secrets / Databricks auth ---
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.databrickscfg
*.pem
*.key

# --- Databricks bundle state ---
.databricks/

# --- macOS / editors ---
.DS_Store
.idea/
.vscode/
!.vscode/settings.json
